        "**The words are used interchangeably in practice, but the examiner expects the distinction stated** - it is a one-mark opening line. [Dhingra 8e]",
        "**Cricothyroidotomy goes through the cricothyroid membrane between thyroid and cricoid cartilages** - superficial, avascular, above the isthmus. [ATLS 10e]",
        "**It takes under a minute, which is why it is the correct emergency surgical airway** and a tracheostomy is not. [ATLS 10e]",
        "**Convert or close it within about 3-7 days because of subglottic stenosis risk**, though the evidence for that timing is weak and practice varies. [ATLS 10e]",
        "**A struggling hypoxic patient is safest managed by intubation or cricothyroidotomy first** - a tracheostomy takes minutes he does not have. [ATLS 10e]",
        "**Then do a controlled tracheostomy over an endotracheal tube** - a secured airway lets the operation proceed unhurried and bloodless. [Dhingra 8e]",
        "**Anatomical dead space falls by about half, roughly 70-100 mL of the usual 150 mL** - the single largest physiological gain. [Dhingra 8e]",
        "**Airway resistance and the work of breathing fall**, because a short wide tube replaces the long narrow upper airway. [Dhingra 8e]",
        "**Secretions can be suctioned directly and ventilation bypasses the larynx** - which halves the anatomical dead space as well. [Dhingra 8e]",
        "**A cuff protects the lower airway from aspiration** - which is the reason for tracheostomy in bulbar palsy and in coma. [Dhingra 8e]",
        "Practical gains: **less sedation, earlier weaning, oral feeding and speech** - a tube in the neck needs no sedation to tolerate. [Bailey and Love 28e 2023]",
